f friends remaining friends when  they're physically separated for a long  period and that leads rather naturally  to a question about the implications for  social capital of more and more social  interaction being done over screens of  one kind or another whether it's  teleconferencing or the cellular phone  or simply internet conversation as  opposed to personal conversation or even  voice conversation so I just like to see  what where you would take that one  speech that I give on that on that topic  no it's it seems to me you're you know  you're absolutely right that you know  there's a kind of technologist take on  this which just says you know you hook  everybody up with enough bandwidth and  and that creates social interaction but  I think that you know the truth of the  matter is that the deeper forms of  social action interaction are based on  reciprocity you know that is to say not  just having things in common you know  interest and so forth but actually you  know the exchange of favors that is  really the basis of friendship and a lot  of that really has to be done you know  face to face Plus which there are all  sorts of just cognitively a lot of clues  that you get about the trustworthiness  and character of other people that you  really only get by by direct contact and  so that's why actually paradoxically  airline travel has not gone down as the  Internet has expanded it's actually gone  up in tandem because the electronic  connections I think stimulate also a  greater need for you know for  face-to-face connections I think the  important  that is not ever going to disappear  McKenna College in thanking professor  Fukuyama

