Fukuyama as teacher of  evil now I don't know whether this means  that I'm stirring up healthy debate or  I'm actually just really so blindly  middle-of-the-road that nobody can find  anything of worth in in what I say but I  do want to touch on I think what are  some of the major social and moral  issues that have convulsed not just our  society but many other societies over  the past generation and what I call the  great disruption and it's a story about  the decline and I believe the rise of  social order there has been a huge  change in moral values in social norms  that govern the way that people relate  to each other in modern societies and I  believe that many of those changes that  have occurred over the past 30 or 40  years in fact were very problematic ones  that that led to societies being able to  do things less effectively in terms of  raising children producing goods and  services and in acting as a political  community and so in some sense  conservatives are right when they talk  about moral decline but I think it's  also the case that the explanations that  are given for why this happened leave  something to be desired and moreover it  seems to me that the trend is really not  one of secular decline from some period  where moral values were  were inevitably better but in fact  follows a more cyclical kind of rhythm  that this was in fact a disruption that  was driven by certain forces but in a  sense is in the process of healing and  so part of the message is in a certain  sense an Aristotelian one that I think  that we went through a period that we  have discussed in in what have been  called the culture wars in in our  society a great moral uncertainty but  that there are reasons for thinking that  we're going out of this period now that  societies are becoming our society and  other societies are becoming more  orderly and what I'd like to do is after  describing this great disruption to talk  a little bit on a kind of more  philosophical plane about where social  order comes from because I think in a  sense we have within us and as a part of  our natural Constitution a certain  desire for order that in a sense will  tend to correct these problems as they  happen now before I go on I want to  define one concept which is critical to  my framework which is the concept of  social capital social capital is a term  that has been used by social scientists  in the last 10 or 15 years to you know  it's a you can think of it as a kind of  utilitarian understanding of the  importance of culture and it's important  to think of it in those terms because of  the legacy of cultural relativism we  tend to think of cultural values as kind  of arbitrary rules that vary from one  society to another and it's impossible  to make judgments as to what is better  and what is worse but in fact social  capital is the or values and shared  culture more generally is the basis of  social cooperation in any society and as  virtually anything in economics and  politics depends on the cooperation of  more than one individual it is the key  to both successful liberal democracy and  to modern economic development and  therefore there's a cultural element to  all of our modern societies that we need  to pay attention to we need to look to  the sources of social capital where  people can actually work together  cooperatively in in groups and  associations to promote various  causes gentlemen my table it introduced  me have just been to Washington at the  the IMF World Bank meetings and you know  that is an example in a certain sense of  civil society in action the ability of  people to associate for for political  ends and that is I think actually  critical to the success of democracy so  we have to worry about this tendency or  the ability to to cooperate now what was  the great disruption in a sense this is  the playing out of a lot of the themes  that have been around ever since the  beginning of the Enlightenment and it  reflects in a way the dilemma of liberal  individualism the Enlightenment was  designed to set people free from  tradition from obscurantist religion  from a lot of irrational traditions that  had bound them to families to workplaces  to to political units and the idea you  know in essence the the modern project  was based on the idea that individuals  should be free to choose all of those  attachments voluntarily and to create a  society based on rational Association  for ends that people chose themselves  and the political expression of this was  of course a modern liberal democracy the  problem as any number of social critics  like Alistair McIntyre Peter Peter  Berger and others had pointed out is  that that liberal individualism tends to  run to an extreme that undermines the  possibility for any form of community  whatsoever so that even the kinds of  voluntary associations that we enter  into marriages employ employment even  citizenship in national units tends  itself to be a weakened because the  strength of our attachments is in a  sense hollowed out by a belief in the  primacy of the individual over the  various communities and forms of  authority to which individuals are  subject now I believe that you can  document this decline of community and  community authority and the rise of  liberal individualism in any number of  ways in my book I actually picked three  of them which have to do with  and you can think of them in a certain  sense as negative measures of social  capital if social capital our shared  values that allow people to cooperate  productively in groups then in a certain  sense crime family breakdown and the  rise of social distrust would all be  empirical indicators statistics that  would tell you that people were less  able to cooperate in the basic  institutions that made up a society I  began this project maybe four or five  years ago collecting data on these three  categories you could pick others you  could pick you know something like  litigation drug abuse suicide rates and  and others I have certain reasons for  focusing on these because the data was  available it was good for the set of  countries that I wanted to look at and  the like but what really struck me  putting all of this together was that  there was a phenomenon that was broad  and extended across societies and was  very concentrated in time that in a  sense begged a certain explanation for  example crime rates we are all aware of  in the United States started to rise in  the approximately in the mid 1960s up  until the early 1990s and most Americans  I'm sure where that American crime rates  are significantly higher than those in  other industrialized countries what many  Americans don't understand is a crime  rates even though starting from a lower  position in in countries like Sweden or  the United Kingdom or Italy also rose at  a very rapid rate over pretty much the  same time period and in fact for the  group of countries that I looked at  which are the developed countries in the  OECD except for the Asian members that  is to say Japan and Korea if you look at  their charts they all move pretty much  in the same direction at the same time  as in the United States the number of  them that experienced these large  increases in crime have come down in  some cases fairly dramatically in the  1990s but that's a story resolved for  later in the speech similarly if you put  together statistics on family and  kinship you see them moving again in a  similar direction fertility drops  very dramatically to sub-replacement  levels in the post-war period to the  point that most and if you say that the  basic role of the family is to promote  reproduction there's not very much that  going on in the developed world the  total fertility rate in a country like  Italy or Germany is down to about 1.1 or  1.2 which means they lose 30 percent of  their population over the next  generation and there's been furthermore  arise I think that other people are  familiar with in things like divorce  children growing up in single-parent  families and the like and again this is  not unique to the United States in  Sweden there well in the United States  about a third of all children currently  are born to single mothers in Sweden  that number is is double two-thirds  although it's a little bit different in  Europe because there's such a high rate  of cohabitation that actually a lot of  those children are growing up in  households with their biological parents  but given the instability of family  relations which is is as far as anyone  can tell just as high in Europe as in  the United States and the substitution  of cohabitation for marriage it's very  clear that very very major changes are  going on in what is by most accounts the  most basic social institution in society  finally you get to the issue of trust  there's no good way of measuring levels  of trust our surveys like the world  value survey that's done at the  University of Michigan that tries to ask  people who whom they trust or what kind  of trust they have in various  institutions and again it's quite  remarkable over this same period there  was a decline in trust particularly for  traditional sources of authority like  the government the police the  bureaucracy the civil service political  parties and the like that again covered  pretty much the same group of countries  now the big issue and I think that you  know in in saying this probably the only  new thing that I'm telling a lot of you  is that this was going on outside of the  United States because I think that any  American who has lived through this  period was aware that there is all of  this social turmoil going on but the  fact that it was going on in other  societies I think implies certain things  about how we can interpret the causes of  it  because Americans tend to be somewhat  parochial they say well we had this big  rise of family breakdown and crime and  so forth which was due to things  happening in American history of Vietnam  Watergate Reaganism I mean everyone's  got their own list of things that  promote distrust and and social social  conflict but if that's in fact the case  it does not explain why this was  happening in New Zealand or Iceland or  any number of other countries that also  experienced this kind of decrease in in  social capital and and and social order  and I think that in many ways a lot of  the existing explanations for that are  out there that have been put up by both  liberals and conservatives capture part  of the truth but really only part of the  truth when you look at the phenomenon as  a larger whole let me begin with the  liberal interpretation of of what went  on I think many people on the Left when  the great disruption began to happen in  the 1960s when crime and family  breakdown began to accelerate at first  simply denied that there was a problem  they would say things like well you know  what is a family anyhow the Jimmy Carter  had a commission on the family that met  and broke up in disagreement because  people said well we can't simply you  know we we don't know what a family is  you know maybe a single-parent family is  just as good a family as a two-parent  family and the like by the 1980s it  seems to me it was too hard to ignore  the fact that these social indicators  were moving in a very negative direction  with a lot of consequences in terms of  welfare and poverty and a lot of other  social interrelated social pathologies  but I think the typical explanation for  this on the left has been that this is  the product of economic inequality or  absent economic opportunities or the  like now there's clearly an economic  basis and in fact my own interpretation  borrows to some extent from that but I  think that the crude form of this simply  does not you know stand a basic and  caracal test because among other things  it doesn't explain why this was going on  in a place like Sweden  the typical answer on on the part of the  left was you know the United States did  not have a big enough welfare state to  buffer poor people against job loss and  economic change and the like which would  imply that all of the social democratic  states in Scandinavia that did do this  should not be suffering from this but as  I indicated before that for example the  rate of family breakdown was as in many  ways as great in Scandinavia in a  country like Sweden as it was in the  United States conversely countries like  Switzerland or Japan which have the  lowest or smallest state sectors of any  industrialized countries actually  suffered the least from from a lot of  these problems and so there's no obvious  correlation between social programs  designed to aid or ameliorate the  problem and and the final outcomes now  there are I think really two  conservative interpretations of this  conservative interpretation number one  was in a way the mirror image of the  liberal interpretation which was to say  it was the state that was involved but  in a negative sense and this was the  this was the interpretation that was put  forward by charles murray in his book a  losing ground that came out in the early  1980s where he argued that it was  actually the welfare state itself that  was promoting family breakdown and crime  by being you know soft on crime and by a  program like eight two families with  dependent children subsidizing a  legitimacy you lose your welfare benefit  if you marry the father of your child if  you're a poor woman and and so forth and  I think that this was in fact a major  theme in American politics it was one of  the motives for Congress passing the  personal opera sponsibility and work  opportunity reconciliation Act of 1996  which was otherwise known as the big  welfare reform act it abolished the AFDC  entitlement that was passed by the  Republican Congress and signed by  President Clinton because the feeling  was that our social policy was actually  contributing to the very problem that it  was meant to solve having been through  you know the raft of econometric studies  that had been done that try to link  afdc to family breakdown I think that  you can say that it was a factor but not  the most important most of these studies  show that something like 15% of the  variance in a family breakdown could  have been attributable to American  welfare policy more than that and  furthermore if you look at the totality  of what was going on in Western families  at this time it's clear that welfare  policy couldn't begin to explain why  there was so much cohabitation in place  of marriage by professional and and  well-to-do couples were getting divorced  why there was so much turmoil in the  American family conservative explanation  number two is in a circumstance more  powerful and that explanation laid these  changes primarily to the door of culture  and there are many different versions of  this one I mean this is a something of a  caricature but you know a lot of  conservatives will say well people just  went crazy in the 1960s you had this  thing's counterculture where people  decided that they're gonna throw off you  know every form of obligation and and  community responsibility and this was  linked to secular humanism or the  decline of religion or traditional  values or something of the sort now and  and conversely to them it implies that  the solution to these problems is simply  a recovery of those kinds of traditional  values that existed at the time prior to  the great disruption now in a certain  sense I think it is indisputable that  there was a cultural basis or background  for all of this stuff happening as I  said in in many respects the kind of  expression of individual assertion self  assertion against community authority is  in a sense embedded in modern liberalism  and embedded in the whole set of modern  Enlightenment institutions that have  been with us for the last 300 years but  that's precisely the problem with this  as an explanation for why this all  happens in the 1960s because in fact if  these ideas have been in the air for the  last 300 years why did it happen  in such a concentrated time across this  particular set of countries in the late  20  century why didn't it happen you know in  fact there was a mini feminist and  sexual revolution that took place in the  1920s why didn't it blossom into  something broader or why did it have to  wait until the 1960s and 70s to really  take root and so I don't and and in the  case of the liberal account and in the  case of both of the conservative  accounts I think that there is an  element of truth in all of them but as  total explanations for this interrelated  set of events I think that there is  something that is that they leave to be  desired and and and in particular with  regard to the timing you know the ratio  for example of divorced married people  increases fourfold in a 25 year period  during the great disruption if you think  about it in terms of general cultural  change that's an enormous shift in  indicators and there are very few  societies other than very rapidly  developing third world countries where  you get similar shifts in deeply rooted  kinds of cultural patterns and and ob8  behavioral habits now my explanation  that I put forward in in the book is  that this really has to do very broadly  with factors not related to American  history and American politics not simply  to a general loss of traditional values  but really by several technological and  economic forces that began to get  underway as part of the general shift  from the industrial era into what was  once called by Daniel Bell the  post-industrial age and now we probably  refer to more commonly as the  Information Age of the information  society this is a society in which in  essence mental labor replaces at the  margin of physical labor in which  knowledge becomes far more important as  a component of of physical output in  which information is really embedded in  all of the material products that we  that we use and manufacture and it had a  whole series of important technological  and  the social ramifications let's take just  the example of crime in a certain sense  to produce an information society you  need intensive individualism it's not a  an accident that Silicon Valley in many  respects is the most competitive and in  certain ways socially atomized parts of  the country with people in you know  leaving companies and no loyalty and  intense competition and it's not  surprising that you celebrate that kind  of rule breaking and individualism you  know you you celebrate Steve Jobs and  Steve Wozniak for designing the Apple  Computer and launching this big  revolution against IBM and the like but  as my colleague at George Mason Seymour  Martin Lipset constantly points out  American individualism is an element of  American exceptionalism that is a  two-edged sword the same person that  does not want to obey the authority of  IBM or AT&amp;T has a counterpart in the you  know the street criminal that doesn't  want to obey the laws of society as he  robs you of your wallet on your way home  from work and in a sense those are two  you know counterparts of the same coin  that the same that there's a good form  of individualism that we celebrate in  terms of technological innovation and a  bad form of individualism that we  celebrate in or we don't celebrate that  we deplore in terms of various forms of  social deviance it's not an accident  that the United States both has the  highest rate of technological innovation  and and you know especially in this past  decade economic growth when compared to  a place like Europe but also highest  rates of divorce of crime of especially  violent crime and other sorts of things  because it reflects the you know the  greater individualism embedded in in our  political values and I think in many  respects this shift to the Information  Age was one of many factors that  accelerated that and had ramifications  in areas like crime in the case of the  family there's a very specific story in  my view that explains why you have these  very dramatic changes in the family that  have to do with - and again if you  remember what you really have to do is  explain why it begins to happen in the  1960s not.you know that that it happens  at all and in my account it really is  due to two separate things as I said the  essence of the Information Age is the  substitution of mental for physical  labor the single most important social  consequence of that was the movement of  millions of women across the van  industrialized world into the paid labor  force and out of and out of households  and I think that it is not surprising  this was obviously an extremely  important social event it created  enormous opportunities for women but it  would be very surprising if it did not  have very destabilizing impact on  families given the traditional role that  women played in families prior to prior  to the great disruption this is not a  quirky theory on my part this is I think  kind of the standard you know  neoclassical economic explanation for  why people get divorced at all you know  there's certain degree of tension in any  marriage relationship but in a certain  sense it's dependent on the woman having  sufficient resources to to make it on on  her own the welfare state for poor women  provided those resources but for  middle-class and-and-and well-to-do  women it was their entry into into the  labor force that that was the permissive  condition for for what happened  the second technological development  that I think occurred at this period was  the birth control pill which I think was  is underestimated in its importance in  its effect on on social norms the birth  control pill was a very contradictory  innovation because by divorcing sex and  reproduction it was supposed to lead to  the liberation of women and in fact was  followed in the United States and