ut of their homes so  there are a lot of possibilities which i  think do involve not you know simply  turning the clock back but but really  doing things differently  yes Studs Terkel  recently Minh maintained that ageism  which I call so now age discrimination  is all too pervasive it is more  pervasive than racism gender ISM and  homophobia it's it's a well-known fact  that so many of us past 40 is out of the  employment and they have a very  difficult time trying to get reemployed  and with that in mind is it very unusual  there that the creme de la creme of the  employment force in Silicon Valley would  have no loyalty at all but make a while  they will have the Sun shines and also  what do you think about these baby  boomers as they need more money  thanks to inflation and all other  expenses so what is going to be the  future you know there's gonna be a lot  of major social changes that will come  about as a pure result of these  demographic changes that are taking  place according to the United Nation  populations office low estimates for  population growth in industrialized  countries the median if you if you carry  those calculations out based on current  trends the median age in a country like  Italy in the year 2050 is going to be 58  in Germany it'll be 54 in Japan it'll be  53 or you know something like so half  the population will be you know will be  older than that and you know with with  better health care obviously people are  going to be a  to work too much later ages than than 65  they already can for all practical  purposes now in public policy this has  been seen largely as a social security  problem which it clearly is I mean  there's a huge social security liability  as the dependency ratio in all of these  Asian countries goes up but there are a  whole series of social problems that we  haven't even begun to address for  example the fact of this kind of normal  succession of people by generations as  you get older you can't any longer take  