u know the breakdown of the  American family because if you look at  who was gaining jobs in the female labor  force in this you know this whole period  you know the vast majority of them were  women in the you know the bottom half of  the income distribution and I think that  actually a great deal of the instability  in the family actually came about in  working-class families which were being  hit in two directions that the the man  were losing you know well-paying factory  jobs in autos or steel or in you know  all those Rust Belt  industries while their girlfriends or  wives were actually finding him you know  a lot of employment and in service  sector kinds of jobs in Walmart's or  banks or or things like that and you  know given I mean the general economic  theory of the family I mean that's a you  know that's a real formula for you know  for trouble and I believe actually that  one of the problems in modern feminism  is that you know in a certain sense the  you know the games from women going to  work in terms of self-esteem and  independence and all those other things  we're much less great for people in the  bottom half of the distribution that is  to say a woman that would have stayed  home with her children and helped  educate and raise them you know it's now  is working for minimum wage at  McDonald's or a Walmart not seeing her  children you know having to find  alternative sources of care it's not  very satisfying the problem is that all  of the books  and the feminist movement is not written  by that kind of woman they're written by  by you know women in the top half of the  distribution that are lawyers or editors  or you know people that have actually  done you know extremely well and so I  think there's a you have to combine  class and gender I think in in these  different ways you know to really  understand part of part of what was  going on there or one more question  Aristotle talks very briefly in the  Nicomachean ethics about the difficulty  of friends remaining friends when  they're physically separated for a long  period and that leads rather naturally  to a question about the implications for  social capital of more and more social  interaction being done over screens of  one kind or another whether it's  teleconferencing or the cellular phone  or simply internet conversation as  opposed to personal conversation or even  voice conversation so I just like to see  what where you would take that one  speech that I give on that on that topic  no it's it seems to me you're you know  you're absolutely right that you know  there's a kind of technologist take on  this which just says you know you hook  everybody up with enough bandwidth and  and that creates social interaction but  I think that you know the truth of the  matter is that the deeper forms of  social action interaction are based on  reciprocity you know that is to say not  just having things in common you know  interest and so forth but actually you  know the exchange of favors that is  really the basis of friendship and a lot  of that really has to be done you know  face to face Plus which there are all  sorts of just cognitively a lot of clues  that you get about the trustworthiness  and character of other people that you  really only get by by direct contact and  so that's why actually paradoxically  airline travel has not gone down as the  Internet has expanded it's actually gone  up in tandem because the electronic  connections I think stimulate also a  greater need for you know for  face-to-face connections I think the  important  that is not ever going to disappear  McKenna College in thanking professor  Fukuyama
 

